The representations produced by a pretrained model are not equally useful for every task. I encountered this in my research on stuttered speech, where an intermediate layer of HuBERT outperformed its final layer at identifying disfluencies. At Samsung Research, I encountered a related challenge while adapting language models to mobile devices: the learning procedure had to be reconsidered for the constraints of the task and hardware. These experiences shaped my interest in designing speech and language models around the representations, adaptation methods, and generation algorithms a task requires. At Carnegie Mellon, I hope to deepen my understanding of language and multimodal modeling while learning to build systems that put these ideas into practice.

My interest in model representations began at IIIT Hyderabad’s Speech Processing Lab under Prof. Anil Kumar Vuppala. I worked on whispered-to-normal speech conversion, where a model must infer missing vocal-fold excitation from the spectral cues that remain in whispered speech. I explored richer spectral representations within HuBERT’s self-supervised pretraining to better capture these cues, improving reconstruction under whispered and noisy conditions. Because their higher dimensionality increased computational cost, I applied spectral smoothing and principal-component selection. The project introduced me to the relationship between the information a representation preserves and the resources needed to use it.

I pursued the question of task-relevant representations in my undergraduate Honours research on distinguishing typical disfluencies from atypical stuttering. Comparing layer-wise representations from self-supervised speech encoders, I found that HuBERT’s fifth layer, out of 24, achieved 97% F1 across disfluency categories and outperformed the final-layer representation. I also contributed to the IIITH-TISA stuttered-speech corpus and co-authored the resulting paper, published at Interspeech 2025. The result showed me that a model’s final representation is not necessarily its most informative one for a particular task. It made me interested in how intermediate representations can guide the design of task-specific speech and language architectures.

To understand how models become usable on different hardware, I joined Samsung Research as an intern working on SnapLite, an on-device runtime serving over 200 production models. Separate vendor runtimes required backend-specific conversion and validation for CPUs, GPUs, and NPUs. I built a unified deployment and verification pipeline that preserved processor-specific kernel optimizations, and added graph partitioning for concurrent execution across two processors. The pipeline reduced time to first inference tenfold and was adopted by multiple product teams. This experience gave me a systems perspective on the decisions involved in moving a model from development to deployment.

After returning to Samsung Research full-time, I worked with Samsung Research Korea on NNTrainer, an open-source generative-AI engine, through a project on privacy-preserving on-device LLM personalization. Conventional fine-tuning of a 1 GB model peaked at 3 GB of memory, making adaptation impractical on the target device. I combined 4-bit base weights with LoRA adapters trained using quantization-aware training, and introduced weight checkpointing and selective activation recomputation. These changes reduced peak memory to under 0.5 GB. To address the remaining execution bottleneck, I designed an asynchronous CPU–NPU dispatch mechanism that overlapped data movement with computation, making training eight times faster than CPU execution. The execution design has been filed for a patent.

This work led me to ask whether the adaptation algorithm itself could be redesigned around the available hardware. GGML’s Llama runtime offered a faster precompiled forward path on the NPU but lacked backward support. I investigated zeroth-order optimization, which estimates updates using forward passes alone. Full-weight perturbations exceeded device memory and dispatch limits, so I restricted updates to a gradient-informed low-rank subspace and used orthogonal projections to retain knowledge across tasks. On a mobile NPU, the method matched or exceeded standard LoRA in accuracy and retention and completed 1,000 steps in 20 minutes—five times faster than prior on-device zeroth-order training. This work is under review at MLSys 2027. I am now investigating a hybrid optimizer in which the device performs local zeroth-order updates while a server periodically provides first-order guidance, raising questions about how communication frequency and stale update directions affect convergence.

My interest in language-model algorithms has also extended from adaptation to generation. When an on-device model escalates a request to a server, processing the conversation context again can add latency and computation. I adapted speculative decoding so that the device continues drafting candidate tokens while the server verifies them in a parallel pass. In early prototype evaluations against full-context server recomputation, this reduced end-to-end latency by 1.4 times and server computation by 2.8 times. The project made me interested in how models with different capabilities can coordinate during generation and how decoding algorithms can use their respective strengths.

More recently, I have been working on multi-turn vision-language model rollouts for DeepEyes using Qwen2.5-VL and vLLM. By caching multimodal preprocessing and optimizing rollout-side CPU processing, I reduced rollout-worker time by 20–30% on tested image sets while preserving generated token streams in paired comparisons. I am now investigating crop reuse and speculative decoding to reduce repeated visual processing and accelerate response generation. This work has made me interested in how decoding methods can be adapted to language generation conditioned on visual inputs, while preserving the behavior of the original model.
My experiences have prepared me to build and adapt models, but they have also shown me where I want a broader foundation. At Carnegie Mellon, Advanced Natural Language Processing would help me develop a more systematic understanding of language tasks and modeling, while Inference Algorithms for Language Modeling would allow me to study how decoding choices affect generation quality and computation. I am also interested in Multimodal Machine Learning as a way to understand representation learning and alignment across visual and linguistic inputs. Together, this coursework would help me move from adapting methods for particular constraints toward reasoning more broadly about why those methods work and how to evaluate them across tasks.

I am drawn to Prof. Emma Strubell’s research on the parameters, learning procedures, and architectural choices needed for effective language models under computational constraints. Her questions connect to my work on layer-wise representations and low-rank adaptation. Prof. Lei Li’s work on parallel language generation, including non-autoregressive translation with layer-wise predictions, connects to my interest in intermediate representations and decoding. I am also interested in Prof. Shinji Watanabe’s research on learned speech representations and end-to-end speech processing, which would let me build on my experience with HuBERT while studying a broader range of speech tasks.

MIIS’s faculty-supervised directed study and collaborative capstone are especially appealing because they would let me apply these foundations to a complete language or multimodal system. In such a setting, I hope to learn how to move from a modeling question to an experimental design: identifying the information a task requires, selecting and evaluating representations and learning methods, and understanding the computational choices needed to put them into practice.

Alongside research, I led six undergraduates in developing Waveform-Wizard, an open-source Python platform that replaced our lab’s proprietary MATLAB speech-analysis workflows and was presented at ICASSP 2025. I also served as an undergraduate teaching assistant for Real Analysis and Linear Algebra. I hope to bring this collaborative experience to MIIS while learning from peers who approach language technologies from different perspectives.

My goal is to become a research engineer developing speech, language, and multimodal models whose design begins with the needs of the task and extends through practical deployment. MIIS would help me grow from an engineer experienced in adapting models into a researcher better equipped to understand their representations, evaluate their behavior, and design the algorithms that make them useful.
